## What happened

In **.NET 11**, Apple dropped DSA support from its crypto libraries, so .NET removed DSA on macOS. Calling `DSA.Create()` now throws a `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected.

## How to fix it

Since you only **verify** legacy DSA signatures, you have two options:

### Option A: Platform-guard DSA (keep verifying on Windows/Linux)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA verification not supported on macOS.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

### Option B: Migrate to ECDSA or RSA (recommended long-term)

Re-sign or re-issue with a modern algorithm and verify with that instead:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

**Bottom line:** DSA is a legacy algorithm. If you can re-sign the data with ECDSA/RSA, do that. If you must keep verifying old DSA signatures, restrict that code path to Windows/Linux with a runtime platform check.
